{"results":[{"url":"https://www.oregonlive.com/wildfires/2026/08/man-accused-of-setting-eastern-oregon-wildfires-seeks-reduction-of-750000-bond.html","final_url":"https://www.oregonlive.com/wildfires/2026/08/man-accused-of-setting-eastern-oregon-wildfires-seeks-reduction-of-750000-bond.html","title":"Man accused of setting eastern Oregon wildfires seeks reduction of $750,000 bond","description":"A Ontario man was charged in connection with numerous fires set around Malheur County. ","language":"en","text":"# Man accused of setting eastern Oregon wildfires seeks reduction of $750,000 bond\n\n* Updated: Aug. 13, 2026, 10:25 a.m.\n* Published: Aug. 13, 2026, 10:03 a.m.\n\nA cockpit view from a U.S. Air Force Reserve C-130H Hercules shows smoke from the Cow Camp complex fire as the aircraft provides aerial firefighting support in eastern Oregon, about 25 miles northwest of Vale near the Baker-Malheur county line, on July 21, 2026.Courtesy of the U.S. Air Force Reserve’s 302nd Airlift Wing\n\nBy \n\n* Tarek Anthony | The Oregonian/OregonLive\n\nA Malheur County man accused of intentionally setting a series of fires that burned thousands of acres of Bureau of Land Management land was indicted last week on nine counts of arson.\n\nA grand jury indicted Paul Emerson, 61, of Ontario, on nine counts of first-degree arson for allegedly setting fires near Lytle Road on at least six different dates between the beginning of June and his arrest on July 22.\n\nProsecutors allege Emerson set four fires the day before his arrest, the largest of which burned more than 6,000 acres and forced local evaucations. The fires were in the area of the Cow Camp Complex, which burned more than 8,000 acres of BLM land. Emerson is accused of causing hundreds of thousands of dollars in damage to public lands.\n\nEmerson had been scheduled to enter a plea in Malheur County court Wednesday, but the proceedings were postponed. He is now scheduled to enter a plea Sept. 9 and is due back in court Friday for a hearing on his request to reduce his $750,000 bond.\n\nEmerson was arrested in July and initially charged with 15 counts of second-degree arson for allegedly setting fires on the BLM land near Vale in the far eastern part of the state.\n\nAt the time of his arrest, Emerson worked one day a week as a delivery driver for the Argus Observer, a local newspaper in Ontario, according to a release recommendation report obtained by the Oregon Roundup Foundation through a public records request.\n\nThe Argus Observer initially reported that Emerson was an employee of the newspaper. The newspaper did not immediately respond to a request for comment Thursday.\n\n“The Malheur County Sheriff’s Office recognizes the significant damage these fires have caused our community and appreciates the public’s support and patience throughout this process,” the agency wrote following Emerson’s arrest.\n\nTarek Anthony\n\nTarek Anthony was a Charles Snowden Program intern with The Oregonian/OregonLive. He graduated from the University of Oregon, where he earned dual degrees in Journalism and Political Science. In 2026, he was...\n\nTAnthony@oregonian.com","author":"Tarek Anthony","published_date":"2026-08-13T17:03:59.582Z","latency_ms":23502.80536699998,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}